==Personal life==
Grandin has noted in her autobiographical works that autism affects every aspect of her life. She has to wear comfortable clothes to counteract her [[sensory processing disorder]] and has structured her lifestyle to avoid sensory overload. She regularly takes [[antidepressant]]s, but no longer uses her [[squeeze machine]],<ref name="Grandin" /> stating in February 2010 that: "It broke two years ago, and I never got around to fixing it. I'm into hugging people now."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://content.time.com/time/arts/article/0,8599,1960347,00.html |title=Temple Grandin on ''Temple Grandin'' |first=Claudia |last=Wallis |date=February 4, 2010 |magazine=[[Time Magazine]] |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=October 11, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201011232031/http://content.time.com/time/arts/article/0,8599,1960347,00.html/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

While enrolled in boarding school, Grandin chose to live a [[Celibacy|celibate]] life.<ref name="AnthropologistOnMars" />{{rp|285}} During a 2013 interview with ''[[The New York Times]]'', she stated "Now I'm old enough to where sexual urges are all gone, and it's like, good riddance."<ref name=GoldmanNYT/>

==Honors==
In 2010, Grandin was named in the ''[[Time 100]]'' list of the one hundred most influential people in the world, in the "Heroes" category.<ref name="hauser" /> In 2011, she received a [[Double Helix Medal]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://doublehelixmedals.cshl.edu/history_11.html |title=Double Helix Medals of 2011 |publisher=Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory |access-date=April 14, 2019}}</ref> She has received honorary degrees from many universities including [[McGill University]] in Canada (1999), and the [[Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences]] (2009), [[Carnegie Mellon University]] in the United States (2012), [[Emory University]] (2016), and [[North Carolina State University]] (2018)<ref>{{Cite web |title=Honorary Degrees Conferred |url=https://leadership.ncsu.edu/board-of-trustees/honorary-degrees/degrees-conferred/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250418211906/https://leadership.ncsu.edu/board-of-trustees/honorary-degrees/degrees-conferred/ |archive-date=2025-04-18 |access-date=2026-06-11 |website=University Leadership |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.grandin.com/professional.resume.html |title=Temple Grandin Ph.D. |first=Temple |last=Grandin |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=February 2, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190202104025/http://grandin.com/professional.resume.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2023, Grandin was awarded an honorary [[Doctor of Science]] degree by [[Iowa State University]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.news.iastate.edu/news/2023/01/26/templegrandinlecture|title=Autism and animal behavior expert to deliver lecture at Iowa State • News Service • Iowa State University|website=www.news.iastate.edu}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Miller |first1=Vanessa |title=University of Iowa, Iowa State bestowing 4 honorary degrees |url=https://www.thegazette.com/higher-education/university-of-iowa-iowa-state-bestowing-four-honorary-degrees/ |website=www.thegazette.com |access-date=May 14, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230227133833/https://www.thegazette.com/higher-education/university-of-iowa-iowa-state-bestowing-four-honorary-degrees/ |archive-date=February 27, 2023 |language=en |date=February 27, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> and was later awarded an honorary [[Doctor of Veterinary Medicine]] from [[Kansas State University]]. This recognition is her first DVM.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.k-state.edu/media/newsreleases/2023-05/Temple-Grandin-honorary-degree51623.html|title=K-State veterinary college presents Temple Grandin with honorary degree|website=www.k-state.edu }}</ref>

In 2011, Grandin was awarded the [[Ashoka (non-profit organization)|Ashoka]] Fellowship.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Temple Grandin {{!}} Ashoka |url=https://www.ashoka.org/en-us/fellow/temple-grandin |access-date=2025-01-17 |website=www.ashoka.org |language=en-us}}</ref> In 2012, Grandin was inducted into the [[Colorado Women's Hall of Fame]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cogreatwomen.org/project/temple-grandin/|title=Temple Grandin|website=Colorado Women's Hall of Fame|date=December 6, 2013 |access-date=November 30, 2019|archive-date=July 16, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200716165447/https://www.cogreatwomen.org/project/temple-grandin/|url-status=live}}</ref> the Texas Trail of Fame,<ref>{{cite web |title=Temple Grandin |url=http://texastrailoffame.org/inductees/temple-grandin/ |website=Western Heritage from the Texas Trail of Fame |access-date=April 14, 2019 |date=June 14, 2013 |archive-date=July 3, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190703130407/http://texastrailoffame.org/inductees/temple-grandin/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and the [[Hall of Great Westerners]] of the [[National Cowboy & Western Heritage Museum]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Hall of Great Westerners |url=https://nationalcowboymuseum.org/hall-of-great-westerners/ |website=National Cowboy & Western Heritage Museum |access-date=November 22, 2019 |archive-date=April 19, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190419213638/https://nationalcowboymuseum.org/hall-of-great-westerners/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Grandin received a Meritorious Achievement Award from the [[World Organisation for Animal Health]] (OIE) in 2015.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.oie.int/for-the-media/press-releases/detail/article/discussion-and-adoption-of-new-intergovernmental-standards-and-guidelines-to-protect-and-improve-ani/ |title=Opening of the 83rd General Session of the World Assembly of Delegates of the World Organisation for Animal Health |website=The World Organisation for Animal Health (OIE) |date=May 24, 2015 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150920113956/http://www.oie.int/for-the-media/press-releases/detail/article/discussion-and-adoption-of-new-intergovernmental-standards-and-guidelines-to-protect-and-improve-ani/ |archive-date=September 20, 2015}}</ref> Also in 2015, she was named an [[honorary fellow]] of the [[Society for Technical Communication]].<ref>{{cite web |publisher=[[Society for Technical Communication]] |url=https://www.stc.org/notebook/2015/05/11/recognition-monday-temple-grandin-named-honorary-fellow/ |title=Recognition Monday: Temple Grandin Named Honorary Fellow |access-date=April 14, 2019 |date=May 11, 2015 |archive-date=August 9, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200809074535/https://www.stc.org/notebook/2015/05/11/recognition-monday-temple-grandin-named-honorary-fellow/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2016, Grandin was inducted into the [[American Academy of Arts and Sciences]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.amacad.org/news/american-academy-arts-and-sciences-elects-213-national-and-international-scholars-artists |title=American Academy of Arts and Sciences Elects 213 National and International Scholars, Artists, Philanthropists, and Business Leaders |date=April 20, 2016 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |website=American Academy of Arts and Sciences |archive-date=August 4, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804174213/https://www.amacad.org/news/american-academy-arts-and-sciences-elects-213-national-and-international-scholars-artists |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2017, Grandin was inducted into the [[National Women's Hall of Fame]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.localsyr.com/news/local-news/ten-women-added-to-national-womens-hall-of-fame-in-seneca-falls/812712663 |title=Ten women added to National Women's Hall of Fame in Seneca Falls |work=WSYR |date=September 17, 2017 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=November 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181106204644/https://www.localsyr.com/news/local-news/ten-women-added-to-national-womens-hall-of-fame-in-seneca-falls/812712663 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://source.colostate.edu/temple-grandin-named-national-womens-hall-fame/ |title=Temple Grandin named to the National Women's Hall of Fame |last=Dimas |first=Jennifer |newspaper=Source |date=February 10, 2017 |publisher=Colorado State University |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=April 30, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190430071145/https://source.colostate.edu/temple-grandin-named-national-womens-hall-fame/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.denverpost.com/2017/02/18/temple-grandin-national-womens-hall-of-fame/ |title=Temple Grandin will be inducted into National Women's Hall of Fame, Q&A |last=Sanchez |first=Hayley |date=February 18, 2017 |work=The Denver Post |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=April 15, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190415183900/https://www.denverpost.com/2017/02/18/temple-grandin-national-womens-hall-of-fame/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On November 15, 2025, Grandin was honored with the [[National Portrait Gallery (United States)|National Portrait Gallery]]'s Portrait of a Nation Award. The Award recognizes the honoree "for their transformative contributions to American history and culture"<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2025-11-17 |title=Smithsonian’s National Portrait Gallery Honors Jamie Dimon, Temple Grandin, Joy Harjo and Steven Spielberg at the 2025 Portrait of a Nation Gala |url=https://npg.si.edu/about-us/press-release/smithsonian-national-portrait-gallery-2025-gala |access-date=2025-12-04 |website=npg.si.edu |language=en}}</ref> and was presented to her by [[Claire Danes]]. The National Portrait Gallery commissioned artist David Lenz to paint Grandin's portrait for inclusion in the Gallery's collection.

==Media==
Grandin has been featured on major media programs, such as Lisa Davis' ''It's Your Health'', [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]]'s ''[[Primetime Live]]'', the ''[[Today (U.S. TV program)|Today Show]]'', ''[[Larry King Live]]'', and ''[[Fresh Air]] with [[Terry Gross]]''. She has been written up in [[Time (magazine)|''Time'' magazine]], [[People (magazine)|''People'' magazine]], [[Discover (magazine)|''Discover'' magazine]], ''[[Forbes]]'', and ''[[The New York Times]]''.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.templegrandin.com/templehome.html |title=Temple Grandin, Ph.D. |publisher=TempleGrandin.com |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=March 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190322082912/http://www.templegrandin.com/templehome.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://discovermagazine.com/2005/may/what-do-animals-think/ |title=What Do Animals Think? |work=Discover Magazine |date=May 1, 2005 |first=Verlyn |last=Klinkenborg |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=April 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190422012635/http://discovermagazine.com/2005/may/what-do-animals-think |url-status=live }}</ref> She was the subject of the ''[[Horizon (BBC TV series)|Horizon]]'' documentary "The Woman Who Thinks Like a Cow", first broadcast by the [[BBC]] on (2006), and ''[[Nick News with Linda Ellerbee]]'' (2006).<ref name="BBC">{{cite web |url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sn/tvradio/programmes/horizon/broadband/tx/temple/ |title=The Woman who thinks like a Cow |date=November 2006 |work=Horizon |publisher=BBC |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=February 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226044354/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sn/tvradio/programmes/horizon/broadband/tx/temple/ |url-status=live }}</ref> She also was the subject of the first episode in the series ''[[First Person (2000 TV series)|First Person]]'' by [[Errol Morris]].

Grandin is the focus of a semi-biographical [[HBO]] film entitled ''[[Temple Grandin (film)|Temple Grandin]]'',<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.beefmagazine.com/cowcalfweekly/1031-temple-grandin-hbo-biopic |title=Temple Grandin Talks About Her Upcoming HBO Biopic |last=Roybal |first=Joe |website=BEEF Magazine |date=October 31, 2008 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=August 6, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806220335/https://www.beefmagazine.com/cowcalfweekly/1031-temple-grandin-hbo-biopic/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.premiumhollywood.com/2010/02/04/tca-tour-a-chat-with-temple-grandin/ |title=A Chat with Temple Grandin |last=Harris |first=Will |date=April 2, 2010 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |publisher=premiumhollywood.com |archive-date=July 12, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190712060704/http://www.premiumhollywood.com/2010/02/04/tca-tour-a-chat-with-temple-grandin/ |url-status=live }}</ref> starring [[Claire Danes]] as Grandin (2010). It was nominated for 15 [[Primetime Emmy Award]]s and won seven, including [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Television Movie|Outstanding Television Movie]] and [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Lead Actress in a Limited Series or Movie|Outstanding Lead Actress in a Miniseries or Movie]] for Claire Danes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/temple-grandin-scores-big-emmy-win-for-hbo/ |title='Temple Grandin' Scores Big Emmy Win For HBO |website=CBSNews.com |date=August 30, 2010 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=August 8, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200808174423/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/temple-grandin-scores-big-emmy-win-for-hbo/ |url-status=live }}</ref> At the [[68th Golden Globe Awards]] (2011), Claire Danes won the [[Golden Globe Award]] for [[Best Actress – Miniseries or Television Film]].

Grandin was featured in ''Beautiful Minds: A Voyage Into the Brain'', a documentary produced in 2006 by Colourfield Tell-A-Vision, a German company. She was named one of 2010's one hundred most influential people in the world by ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' magazine.<ref name="hauser" /> In 2011, she was featured in an episode of the [[Science (TV network)|Science]] documentary series ''[[Ingenious Minds]]''. In 2018, Grandin was featured in the documentary ''[[This Business of Autism]]'', which explored autism employment and the success story of autism employers such as [[Spectrum Designs Foundation]] and was produced by Mesh Omnimedia.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.broadwayworld.com/bwwtv/article/Photo-Flash-THE-BUSINESS-OF-AUTISM-Debuts-To-Sold-Out-Crowd-20180606 |title=This Business of Autism Debuts to Sold Out Crowd |date=June 6, 2018 |access-date=April 14, 2019 |archive-date=June 28, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180628204940/https://www.broadwayworld.com/bwwtv/article/Photo-Flash-THE-BUSINESS-OF-AUTISM-Debuts-To-Sold-Out-Crowd-20180606 |url-status=live }}</ref>

She was featured in [[Michael Pollan]]'s 2006 book, ''[[The Omnivore's Dilemma]]'',<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.penguin.com/static/pdf/teachersguides/OmnivoresDilemmaTG.pdf|title=A Teacher's Guide to the Omnivore's Dilemma: A Natural History of Four Meals|access-date=April 14, 2019|archive-date=August 9, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200809023510/https://www.penguin.com/static/pdf/teachersguides/OmnivoresDilemmaTG.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> in which she discussed the livestock industry.  [[Oliver Sacks]] included a chapter of his visit with her in his 1995 book ''[[An Anthropologist on Mars]]'', the book's title referring to Grandin's assessment of being puzzled by complex emotions and games people play.  "Much of the time," she told Sacks, "I feel like an anthropologist on Mars."<ref>{{cite book |first=Oliver |last=Sacks |title=An Anthropologist on Mars |isbn=0679437851 |publisher=Vintage Canada |year=1995 |page=259}}</ref>

Folk-punk band [[AJJ (band)|AJJ]] included two songs called "Temple Grandin" and "Temple Grandin Too" on their LP ''[[Christmas Island (Andrew Jackson Jihad album)|Christmas Island]]''. In 2017, Grandin was the focus of a children's book by author Julia Finlay Mosca titled ''The Girl Who Thought In Pictures, A Story of Temple Grandin''.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Mosca |first=Julia |title=The Girl Who Thought In Pictures, The story of Temple Grandin |year=2017 |publisher=Innovation Press |isbn=9781943147304 |oclc=966575841}}</ref>

In 2018, Grandin was profiled in the book ''Rescuing Ladybugs'' by author and animal advocate [[Jennifer Skiff]] as a "global hero" for "standing her ground and fighting for change after witnessing the extreme mistreatment of animals" used in farming.<ref>{{cite book |first=Jennifer |last=Skiff |title=Rescuing Ladybugs: Inspirational Encounters with Animals That Changed the World |isbn=9781608685028 |publisher=New World Library |year=2018 |page=170}}</ref> In 2023, 
author [[Brad Meltzer]] and illustrator [[Chris Eliopoulos]] released the children's book ''I am Temple Grandin'' as a part of the "Ordinary People Change the World" series.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Meltzer |first1=Brad |last2=Eliopoulos |first2=Christopher |date=March 28, 2023 |title=I am Temple Grandin |language=English |publisher=Penguin Random House |page= |isbn=9780593405970 |series=Ordinary People Change the World}}</ref> The book was later adapted into an episode for the [[PBS]] show ''[[Xavier Riddle and the Secret Museum]]''.<ref>{{cite episode |last=Johnston |first=Charles |date=April 5, 2021 |title=I Am Temple Grandin |url= |location= |series=Xavier Riddle and the Secret Museum |season=2 |issue=30 |publisher=PBS |minutes= |time= |network=PBS Kids}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Milligan |first= Mercedes |date=March 31, 2021 |title=PBS KIDS Celebrates Autism Acceptance with New Episodes |url=https://www.animationmagazine.net/2021/03/pbs-kids-celebrates-autism-acceptance-with-new-episodes/ |magazine=[[Animation Magazine]] |access-date=May 30, 2025}}</ref>

